.TH "SKARN-MCP" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-mcp \- Local stdio MCP server: read\-only, redacted scan and config
tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBmcp\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a Model Context Protocol server on stdin and stdout for one client.
Messages are newline\-delimited JSON\-RPC 2.0, one message per line, UTF\-8,
with no embedded newline; a request frame larger than 1048576 bytes is
answered with a single \-32600 error and skipped. The client opens with
initialize and the server replies with the negotiated protocolVersion, a tools
capability, its serverInfo and a short instructions paragraph; tools/list and
tools/call before that reply are answered \-32600, while ping is answered at
any time. Notifications, including notifications/initialized and
notifications/cancelled, are accepted and never answered. An unparseable frame
is \-32700, a frame that is not a JSON\-RPC 2.0 request object is \-32600, an
unknown method is \-32601, and a malformed initialize or an unknown tool name
is \-32602. An argument a tool refuses \- an unknown key, a wrong type, an
out\-of\-range number, a string over 4096 bytes or one carrying control
characters \- comes back as a normal tool result marked isError so the model
can correct itself, never as a protocol error. On first run the End User
License Agreement notice is printed as one line on stderr before the protocol
loop starts and the server proceeds; `skarn eula accept`, or
SKARN_EULA_ACCEPTED=1 in the client's env block, silences it. Nothing but MCP
messages is ever written to stdout. Stderr is not part of the protocol and may
carry a diagnostic from the scan path, for example when the home directory
cannot be resolved; the tool still answers with a normal result marked isError
and the server stays up. Register the server in a client with the command
`skarn` and the argument list ["mcp"], which requires skarn on PATH; the
documented alternative is the version\-pinned form `npx \-y
@skarn\-security/skarn@X.Y.Z mcp`, which downloads that exact version from the
npm registry on first use and caches it. An unpinned npx form is reported as a
finding by `skarn vet`, so pin the version.
.PP
Run a local Model Context Protocol (MCP) server so an AI coding assistant can
call Skarn directly. The transport is stdio: newline\-delimited JSON\-RPC 2.0
on stdin and stdout, one server process per client, and the process exits 0
when the client closes stdin. The negotiated protocol version is 2025\-11\-25.
A client that asks for 2025\-06\-18 gets that version back; any other request,
including the batch\-era 2025\-03\-26 and the stateless 2026\-07\-28, is
answered with 2025\-11\-25. Batched requests are not accepted: a top\-level
JSON array is answered with one \-32600 error. Four read\-only tools are
exposed by default, and \-\-enable\-recall adds search_sessions and
get_session, which return transcript text and are described under that option.
scan_sessions returns the same redacted scan report that `skarn assess
\-\-json` prints, minus the saved_approvals key, minus the per\-incident
context snippet, minus the reach object and minus every transcript\-authored
label, which means the project labels on both the incidents and the attack
chains and the tool name on an incident; it takes severity, hours, project and
user, where user is a comma\-separated list of the user ids or email addresses
a Compliance API export recorded, an id matched exactly and an address
case\-insensitively. Unlike assess, scan_sessions does not mask session fields
with the values vet detects in assistant configuration. vet_configs returns
the masked findings report that `skarn vet \-\-format json` prints and takes
no arguments; those findings quote this machine's own configuration, including
hook command lines and MCP server definitions, with any credential\-shaped
value masked. list_sessions returns recent sessions as identifiers and counts
only \- session id, assistant, the two timestamps, message and tool counts,
token totals, and the user id a Compliance API export recorded \- and takes
hours, limit, project, cli and user. session_stats returns the same aggregate
counts across those sessions and takes hours, project, cli and user. Every
tool is annotated read\-only, non\-destructive and closed\-world. No tool
writes, changes or removes a file, no tool opens a network connection, and no
default tool returns session message text or a label authored inside a
transcript such as a project name, a git branch, a model id or a tool name.
Every value a detector matched is reported only in its masked form, which
keeps at most the first character and last two characters and discards the
middle; a value under 16 characters, and any personal data, is replaced whole.
That is a truncated preview rather than a one\-way transform of the whole
value. Three classes of string are still passed through as they are: a file
path, a session id, and a timestamp. Each is a locator the tools exist to hand
back, and each is read from the transcript or the store rather than generated
by Skarn, so a credential deliberately planted in a session id or a directory
name reaches the client verbatim. A Claude Code store also encodes the project
directory into its transcript file name, so the returned session_file carries
that directory in encoded form. Every other Skarn surface passes those three
through the same way, with one exception: a scan report, scan_sessions
included, prints the file path of a session whose own project, project root or
working directory label stopped a detection rule at its regex work limit as
[withheld: regex work limit] followed by a short hash of the path. The user id
and workspace id a Compliance API export recorded are passed through as
locators too, on list_sessions rows and scan_sessions incidents and chains,
while the email address it recorded never reaches any tool result. Tool
results are returned to the MCP client that spawned the server, which is the
assistant, so they travel wherever that assistant sends its context. The verb
needs no license. vet_configs reads project configuration at the server
process's working directory, at the top of its git repository and, in a linked
worktree, at the main checkout; the client chooses the working directory when
it spawns the server. The tool takes neither \-\-all\-projects nor
\-\-project\-only; use the CLI for either scope. The vet_configs tool refuses
every network root, including plain UNC shares. For a network\-spelled HOME it
reports one NetworkPathRefused gap and reads nothing under that home. Neither
skarn mcp nor skarn serve accepts \-\-evidence\-home. list_sessions,
session_stats, search_sessions and get_session carry tools_dropped,
stores_unreadable and sessions_truncated after compliance_withheld, and
list_sessions, session_stats and search_sessions also carry
session_folders_without_transcript, limited to the hours window; get_session
carries it as 0, because a lookup of one session counts no folders.
list_sessions and session_stats count the sources the cli argument selects,
with sessions_truncated limited to the hours window, and never narrow by
project or user; search_sessions counts every assistant over its hours window,
or over all history when project is given without hours; get_session counts
every assistant over all history and reports sessions_truncated for the
returned session only. A get_session call for a session id that is not found
returns an error whose text is the not\-found message followed by one line for
each non\-zero tools_dropped or stores_unreadable count. scan_sessions keeps
stores_unreadable, sessions_truncated, turns_unavailable and turns_unverified
in its stats beside tools_dropped and sessions_incomplete.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-enable\-recall\fR
Add the content tools search_sessions and get_session to the server. Off by
default. They return transcript content with detected credentials masked and
other text as recorded, masking a detected value in every session of the
response rather than only where it was detected, so anything they return
leaves the machine through the connected MCP client and reaches the model
provider. The search_sessions response carries no echo of the query or of the
project filter, and its skipped count names how many sessions were left out
because their file changed on disk while it was being read; those sessions
contribute no row rather than being masked from stale bytes. When a detection
rule stops at its regex work limit in the masking scan of a session the
response would include, either tool returns the redaction error and no
transcript content, because a value the rule did not finish checking could
otherwise be returned unmasked.
